No source on the May 2026 breach describes a pull_request_target or Pwn-Request mechanism: Grafana says the breach originated from the TanStack npm supply-chain attack orchestrated by TeamPCP, detected on 2026-05-11, and that a GitHub workflow token missed during the rotation that followed gave the attackers access ([The Hacker News, 2026-05-20](https://thehackernews.com/2026/05/grafana-github-breach-exposes-source.html)). The theft was not limited to source code: the downloaded content also included internal repositories holding operational information and business contact names and email addresses, while production systems and the Grafana Cloud platform were not involved ([The Hacker News, 2026-05-20](https://thehackernews.com/2026/05/grafana-github-breach-exposes-source.html)). The link between CoinbaseCartel and ShinyHunters is contested, since ShinyHunters told BleepingComputer the group is not linked to it ([BleepingComputer, 2026-05-18](https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/grafana-says-stolen-github-token-let-hackers-steal-codebase/)), and SecurityWeek counts 105 victims on CoinbaseCartel's site rather than about 170 ([SecurityWeek, 2026-05-18](https://www.securityweek.com/grafana-confirms-breach-after-hackers-claim-they-stole-data/)). No source places Grafana in Swiss or EU public-sector monitoring estates or calls for watching unofficial Grafana plugins; Grafana says it audited all commits for malicious activity ([The Hacker News, 2026-05-20](https://thehackernews.com/2026/05/grafana-github-breach-exposes-source.html)).
